     9	        thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps, lem-tube-lemma-for-a-compact-factor,
    10	        thm-finite-products-of-compact-spaces, thm-alexander-subbase-lemma,
    11	        thm-tychonoff, def-compactness-variants, thm-compactness-variants-hierarchy,
    12	        cor-heine-borel-in-the-product-topology, def-locally-compact-space,
    13	        thm-locally-compact-hausdorff-basics,
    14	        lem-dependent-choice-along-a-sequence-of-relations, def-baire-space,
    15	        thm-baire-category-locally-compact-hausdorff, def-one-point-compactification,
    16	        thm-one-point-compactification-properties, lem-the-order-topology-on-an-ordinal,
    17	        thm-ordinal-spaces-and-compactness,
    18	        thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact,
    19	        thm-quasicomponents-equal-components-in-a-compact-hausdorff-space,
    20	        rem-compactness-conventions-and-choice-ledger]
    21	examples: [fs-a-compact-subset-is-closed-in-every-space,
    22	           fs-compact-implies-sequentially-compact,
    23	           fs-sequentially-compact-implies-compact,
    24	           fs-countably-compact-implies-compact,
    25	           fs-local-compactness-is-hereditary]
    26	---
    27	
    28	**Objective.** Compactness is the hypothesis under which an infinite covering
    29	argument collapses to a finite one. This page defines it for an arbitrary
    30	topological space, proves what it gives — closedness in a Hausdorff space,
    31	separation of disjoint compact sets, attainment of extreme values, automatic
    32	continuity of an inverse — and settles how it behaves under the constructions of
    33	general topology: subspaces, products of any size, continuous images, and the
    34	adjunction of a single point at infinity.
    35	
    36	**Compactness of a subset is intrinsic.** [[def-compact-space]] defines a compact
    37	space by open covers and calls a subset compact when the subspace it carries is
    38	compact. That is a statement about the subspace alone, so the notion does not
    39	depend on which larger space the subset is regarded as sitting in. The bridge to
    40	the working form is [[lem-compactness-of-a-subspace-is-ambient]]: a subset is
    41	compact exactly when every family of ambient open sets covering it has finitely
    42	many members that already cover it, in the indexed form as well as the unindexed
    43	one. Almost every proof below uses the indexed form, because a cover is normally
    44	produced by a rule attaching an open set to each point or to each index, and a
    45	bare set of open sets forgets the rule. The same reading was already fixed for
    46	metric spaces, and [[thm-compactness-agrees-with-metric-compactness]] shows that
    47	the two developments describe one notion, which is what makes the whole metric
    48	theory of compactness available here once a metric inducing the topology is named.
    49	
    50	**What compactness gives.** [[thm-compact-iff-fip]] converts the covering
    51	condition into a statement about closed sets with the finite intersection
    52	property, which is the form used whenever a common point has to be produced.
    53	[[thm-closed-subspace-of-a-compact-space-is-compact]] and
    54	[[thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed]] are the two halves of the
    55	relationship between compactness and closedness, and the second carries more than
    56	its name: in a Hausdorff space a point and a disjoint compact set, and two
    57	disjoint compact sets, lie in disjoint open sets. Those separation clauses are
    58	proved **choice-free**, by collecting the family of all open sets that work
    59	rather than choosing one for each point, and they are what later pages use to
    60	separate closed sets in a compact Hausdorff space.
    61	[[thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps]] collects the payoff: a continuous image
    62	of a compact space is compact, a continuous real-valued function on a nonempty
    63	compact space attains a maximum and a minimum, and a continuous bijection from a
    64	compact space to a Hausdorff space is a homeomorphism.
    65	
    66	**Products.** [[lem-tube-lemma-for-a-compact-factor]] is the step that makes a
    67	product of two compact spaces compact, and it is proved with no choice principle
    68	at all by indexing its cover with pairs of open sets;
    69	[[thm-finite-products-of-compact-spaces]] runs the induction. For an arbitrary
    70	index set the argument changes character: [[thm-alexander-subbase-lemma]] reduces
    71	compactness to covers by members of a fixed subbasis, using Zorn's lemma, and
    72	[[thm-tychonoff]] then follows because a subbasic cover of a product restricts
    73	one coordinate at a time. Tychonoff's theorem implies the Axiom of Choice, so no
    74	proof of it can be free.
    75	
    76	**Weaker cousins, and what each implication costs.**
    77	[[def-compactness-variants]] introduces countable compactness, the Lindelöf
    78	property, sequential compactness, limit point compactness, $\sigma$-compactness
    79	and relative compactness, and identifies those of them that the metric
    80	development had already defined. [[thm-compactness-variants-hierarchy]] proves the
    81	implications one at a time rather than as a single equivalence, because an
    82	equivalence proved round a cycle charges every arrow the maximum: compactness
    83	gives countable compactness, the Lindelöf property and limit point compactness in
    84	ZF; sequential compactness gives countable compactness at the cost of countable
    85	choice; countable compactness gives limit point compactness at the cost of
    86	dependent choice, and the converse holds when singletons are closed, again at
    87	the cost of countable choice.
    88	[[cor-heine-borel-in-the-product-topology]] records that on $\mathbb{R}^n$ the
    89	product topology is the Euclidean one, so Heine-Borel applies unchanged.
    90	
    91	**Local compactness and the Baire property.** [[def-locally-compact-space]] asks
    92	only that every point have a compact neighbourhood, and
    93	[[thm-locally-compact-hausdorff-basics]] shows what a Hausdorff hypothesis adds:
    94	a neighbourhood base of compact sets, the shrinking of any open set around a
    95	point to an open set with compact closure inside it, heredity along open and
    96	closed subspaces, and an open set with compact closure around any compact set.
    97	That shrinking clause is exactly what a nested construction needs, and
    98	[[thm-baire-category-locally-compact-hausdorff]] runs one: every locally compact
    99	Hausdorff space is a Baire space ([[def-baire-space]]), assuming dependent
   100	choice. The construction shrinks at stage $n$ into the $n$-th dense open set, so
   101	the relation governing it changes with the stage;
   102	[[lem-dependent-choice-along-a-sequence-of-relations]] is the bridge from the
   103	single-relation form of dependent choice to that situation, and it is needed
   104	because a family of relations is not a relation.
   105	
   106	**One point at infinity.** [[def-one-point-compactification]] adjoins a single
   107	point, constructed from the space rather than assumed to exist, and declares open
   108	the complements of the closed compact sets; the verification that this is a
   109	topology is carried out there.
   110	[[thm-one-point-compactification-properties]] proves that the result is compact,
   111	that the original space sits inside it as an open subspace with its own topology,
   112	that it is dense exactly when the original space is not compact, and that the
   113	result is Hausdorff exactly when the original space is locally compact and
   114	Hausdorff.
   115	
   116	**Ordinals and the long ray.** [[lem-the-order-topology-on-an-ordinal]] gives an
   117	ordinal space a basis of clopen sets and identifies its isolated points;
   118	[[thm-ordinal-spaces-and-compactness]] shows that a successor ordinal is compact
   119	and a limit ordinal is not, and that $\omega_1$ is sequentially compact and
   120	countably compact without being compact, assuming countable choice.
   121	[[thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact]] proves a Heine-Borel
   122	theorem for the closed initial segments of the long ray, using only order density
   123	and the least upper bound property, and deduces that the ray is not compact — a
   124	theorem of ZF — and, assuming countable choice, that it is countably compact and
   125	not Lindelöf. Both spaces are the standard witnesses for the
   126	false statements that close the page.
   127	[[thm-quasicomponents-equal-components-in-a-compact-hausdorff-space]] is the last
   128	theorem: in a compact Hausdorff space the quasicomponents are connected, so the
   129	two decompositions of a space into connected pieces agree.
   130	
   131	**The ledger.** [[rem-compactness-conventions-and-choice-ledger]] settles the
   132	conventions in force — that *compact* means the covering condition alone and
   133	never includes a Hausdorff hypothesis, that compactness of a subset is intrinsic,
   134	and that relative compactness is the one notion here that is not — and then
   135	records, result by result, which arguments are theorems of ZF and which spend the
   136	Axiom of Choice, countable choice or dependent choice. Every entry is a statement
   137	about the proof given here and never a claim that a choice principle is necessary.
   138	
   139	**False statements** close the page, each with a witness: that a compact
   140	subset is closed in every space, that compactness and sequential compactness
   141	imply each other, that countable compactness implies compactness, and that local
   142	compactness is hereditary. The worked witnesses, together with the compactness of
   143	the interval, the Cantor set and the Hilbert cube, are on the companion page.

