    34	---
    35	## Definition
    36	
    37	Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]).
    38	
    39	- An **open cover** of $(X,\mathcal{T})$ is a family
    40	  $\mathcal{U} \subseteq \mathcal{T}$ of open sets with $X = \bigcup \mathcal{U}$,
    41	  where $\bigcup \mathcal{U} = \{\, x \in X : x \in U \text{ for some } U \in \mathcal{U} \,\}$.
    42	- A **subcover** of $\mathcal{U}$ is a subfamily $\mathcal{V} \subseteq \mathcal{U}$
    43	  that is itself an open cover.
    44	- A family $\mathcal{V}$ of sets is **finite** when $\mathcal{V} = \varnothing$ or
    45	  there are $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and sets $V_0, \dots, V_n$ with
    46	  $\mathcal{V} = \{V_0, \dots, V_n\}$; repetitions in the list are allowed and
    47	  harmless.
    48	- $(X,\mathcal{T})$ is **compact** when every open cover of it has a finite
    49	  subcover: for every open cover $\mathcal{U}$, either $X = \varnothing$ and the
    50	  empty subfamily covers it, or there are $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and
    51	  $U_0, \dots, U_n \in \mathcal{U}$ with
    52	  $$X = U_0 \cup \dots \cup U_n .$$
    53	- A subset $A \subseteq X$ is a **compact subset** of $X$ when the subspace
    54	  $(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$ is a compact topological space, $\mathcal{T}_A$ being the
    55	  subspace topology ([[def-subspace-topology-top]]).
    56	
    57	**Compactness of a subset is defined intrinsically, and only intrinsically.**
    58	The last clause speaks about the subspace $(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$ and its own open
    59	sets, not about families of open subsets of the ambient $X$. The two readings do
    60	agree, but that is a theorem and not a convention: it is
    61	[[lem-compactness-of-a-subspace-is-ambient]], and no item of this library may use
    62	the ambient reading without citing it. Taking the intrinsic reading as the
    63	definition is what makes "compact" a property of the space $(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$
    64	alone, so that a set compact in one ambient space is compact in every other one
    65	in which it sits as a subspace. This is exactly the convention already fixed for
    66	metric spaces by [[def-metric-compactness]], and the agreement of that
    67	definition with this one is [[thm-compactness-agrees-with-metric-compactness]].
    68	
    69	**The empty space is compact**, since the empty subfamily of any family covers
    70	it; this is the reason the clause above is written with the two cases. Every
    71	space listed as $\{x_0, \dots, x_n\}$ is compact too: given a cover, each $x_i$
    72	lies in some member, and finitely many members named in this way already cover.
    73	So every finite space is compact, whatever its topology, and in particular the
    74	discrete topology on a finite set is compact while the discrete topology on an
    75	infinite set is not ([[def-standard-topologies]]).
    76	
    77	**The finiteness convention.** "Finite" above is the listing form. It agrees with
    78	the definition of finiteness by equinumerosity with a natural number
    79	([[def-countable]]), in both directions, and the agreement is the one already
    80	discharged in [[def-metric-compactness]]: a nonempty set equinumerous with
    81	$m \ge 1$ is listable, and a set listed as $\{a_0, \dots, a_n\}$ injects into
    82	$\sigma(n)$ by sending $x$ to the least $i \le n$ with $a_i = x$. Neither
    83	direction uses a choice principle; the second selects nothing, taking a least
    84	index instead.
    85	
    86	**Quasicompact is not used here.** Some authors, following Bourbaki, reserve
    87	*compact* for a space that is both compact in the above sense and Hausdorff, and
    88	call the open-cover condition alone *quasicompact*. This library follows the more
    89	widely adopted convention: **compact** means the open-cover condition and nothing
    90	more, and a Hausdorff hypothesis is always written out. The fork is recorded in
    91	[[rem-compactness-conventions-and-choice-ledger]].
    92	
    93	## Remarks
    94	
    95	**Why open covers rather than covers by arbitrary sets.** Nothing in the
    96	definition would break if $\mathcal{U}$ were allowed to consist of arbitrary
    97	subsets of $X$, but the resulting notion would be uninteresting: every space is
    98	covered by its singletons, and only a finite space would survive. Openness of the
    99	members is what makes the condition a genuine restriction, and it is what
   100	[[lem-compactness-of-a-subspace-is-ambient]] has to keep track of when the
   101	ambient space changes.
   102	
   103	**A warning about the word "cover".** A family may cover $A \subseteq X$ without
   104	being a family of subsets of $A$: the members are open subsets of $X$ and their
   105	union merely contains $A$. That is the ambient reading, and it is a different
   106	statement from "$\mathcal{U}$ is an open cover of the space
   107	$(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$", whose members are open subsets of $A$. Which of the two is
   108	meant is written out everywhere on this page.
