    27	  references:
    28	    - title: "Compact space (Wikipedia)"
    29	      url: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Compact_space"
    30	    - title: "Sierpiński space (Wikipedia)"
    31	      url: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sierpi%C5%84ski_space"
    32	pipeline_run: null
    33	---
    34	
    35	## Statement
    36	
    37	**False claim:** in every topological space $(X, \mathcal{T})$
    38	([[def-topological-space]]), a compact subset ([[def-compact-space]]) is closed.
    39	
    40	**Where the claim comes from, and what is actually true.** In a **Hausdorff**
    41	space a compact subset is closed, and that is
    42	[[thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed]], claim 3. The claim above
    43	is that theorem with its hypothesis dropped. The refutation builds its own
    44	witness: Sierpinski space, the two-point space with exactly one non-trivial open
    45	set ([[def-standard-topologies]]).
    46	
    47	## Facts & Assumptions
    48	
    49	**Given:** The two-element set $S = \{a,b\}$ with $a \ne b$, and the family $\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{Sier}} = \{\varnothing, \{b\}, S\}$.
    50	
    51	[A1] The false claim: in every topological space a compact subset is closed.
    52	
    53	[L1] $\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{Sier}}$ is a topology on $S$, the particular-point topology with particular point $b$; a subset of $S$ is closed exactly when its complement lies in $\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{Sier}}$ ([[def-standard-topologies]], [[def-topological-space]]).
    54	
    55	[L2] A subset $A$ of a space is a compact subset when the subspace $(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$ is compact, and every space listed as $\{x_0, \dots, x_n\}$ is compact ([[def-compact-space]], [[def-subspace-topology-top]]).
    56	
    57	[L3] A space is Hausdorff when distinct points lie in disjoint open sets ([[def-hausdorff-space]]).
    58	
    59	## Refutation
    60	
    61	**Proof technique:** contradiction.
    62	
    63	1.1 Suppose the claim [A1] holds, so that in every topological space every compact subset is closed. [A1, assume-contra]
    64	
    65	1.2 $(S, \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{Sier}})$ is a topological space by [L1], and its closed sets are $S$, $\{a\}$ and $\varnothing$, the complements of $\varnothing$, $\{b\}$ and $S$. [L1]
    66	
    67	2.1 $\{b\}$ is a compact subset of $S$: the subspace it carries is a one-point space, which is compact by [L2]. [L2, step 1.2]
    68	
    69	2.2 $\{b\}$ is not closed in $S$, since its complement $\{a\}$ is not a member of $\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{Sier}}$. [L1, step 1.2]
    70	
    71	3.1 By [A1] applied to the space of step 1.2 and the compact subset of step 2.1, the set $\{b\}$ would be closed, which step 2.2 denies. So the claim [A1] is false. [A1, step 2.1, step 2.2, discharge-contradiction] ∎
    72	
    73	## Remarks
    74	
    75	**The witness is as small as a witness can be.** Sierpinski space has two points and three open sets, and it fails the Hausdorff condition for the only reason available: the only open set containing $a$ is $S$, which also contains $b$ ([[def-hausdorff-space]]). Since every finite space is compact, every subset of it is a compact subset, so the failure is not about compactness being hard to achieve; it is entirely about closedness.
    76	
    77	**What survives without a separation hypothesis.** A compact subset of an arbitrary space is still compact in every space containing it as a subspace, that being the content of the intrinsic definition ([[def-compact-space]]), and a closed subset of a compact space is still compact ([[thm-closed-subspace-of-a-compact-space-is-compact]]). It is only the converse direction, from compact to closed, that needs the ambient space to separate points.

