 {"id":"ex-sigma-compactness-and-lindelof-in-the-line-and-the-rationals","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%CE%A3-compact_space","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lindel%C3%B6f_space"],"rationale":"The sources give the standard sigma-compact and Lindeloef definitions and implications, including the familiar real-line and countable-space examples. The item separates the behaviour of R and Q under its local-compactness convention and supplies explicit interval/countability proofs; those proofs are local. Both URLs were retrieved through the model-side web route (HTTP 200).","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
 {"id":"ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Sorgenfrey_topology"],"rationale":"The Encyclopedia of Mathematics records that the Sorgenfrey line is Lindeloef, its square is not Lindeloef, and the line is not locally compact. The item also proves non-sigma-compactness and expands the standard rational-selection and closed-discrete-diagonal arguments, so its statement and proof are sourced alterations. The URL was retrieved through the model-side web route (HTTP 200).","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
 {"id":"cex-limit-point-compact-without-countable-compactness","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Limit_point_compact"],"rationale":"The source gives the standard doubled-integer construction Z times an indiscrete two-point space as limit-point compact but not countably compact. The item uses N instead of Z and proves the open-set, partner-point and countable-cover claims directly, so the statement is semantically identical up to the discrete countable factor and the proof is local. The URL was retrieved through the model-side web route (HTTP 200).","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
-{"id":"cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=b49ec80d24320c11a1364c9f100fcf5631b20a73&repid=rep1&type=pdf","https://www.math.toronto.edu/ivan/mat327/docs/notes/19-compactifications.pdf"],"rationale":"The open textbook gives the standard quotient R/Z obtained by collapsing all integers to one point and states that it is not locally compact because every neighbourhood of the collapsed point contains an infinite closed discrete subspace; the university notes also record failure of local compactness under continuous images. The item replaces Z by the embedded naturals and expands the same closed-discrete proof, so it is a semantic and proof-level adaptation. Both URLs were retrieved through the model-side web route (HTTP 200).","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
+{"id":"cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=b49ec80d24320c11a1364c9f100fcf5631b20a73&repid=rep1&type=pdf","https://www.math.toronto.edu/ivan/mat327/docs/notes/19-compactifications.pdf"],"rationale":"The open textbook gives the standard quotient R/Z obtained by collapsing all integers to one point and states that it is not locally compact because every neighbourhood of the collapsed point contains an infinite closed discrete subspace; the university notes also record failure of local compactness under continuous images. The item replaces Z by the embedded naturals and expands the same closed-discrete proof, so it is a semantic and proof-level adaptation. Both URLs were retrieved through the model-side web route (HTTP 200); the previously unverified CiteseerX PDF was re-opened successfully through that route at A4 on 2026-08-04.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
 {"id":"ex-the-alexander-subbase-lemma-applied-to-a-closed-interval","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/08ZP","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heine%E2%80%93Borel_theorem"],"rationale":"Stacks gives Alexander's subbase lemma and Heine-Borel confirms compactness of the closed interval. The item is a bespoke worked application to the order-ray subbasis, reducing a cover to two rays via the least-upper-bound property; its conclusion is a sourced standard fact while the particular dependency-labelled verification is local. Both URLs were retrieved through the model-side web route (HTTP 200).","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}

