    39	
    40	## Three conventions, fixed once
    41	
    42	**1. Compact means the open-cover condition and nothing more.** Following
    43	Bourbaki, some authors reserve *compact* for a space that is both quasicompact,
    44	meaning every open cover has a finite subcover, and Hausdorff, and then say
    45	*quasicompact* for the cover condition alone. This library takes the more widely
    46	adopted convention: [[def-compact-space]] defines *compact* as the cover
    47	condition, the word *quasicompact* is not used, and every Hausdorff hypothesis is
    48	written into the statement that needs it — as in
    49	[[thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed]] and
    50	[[thm-locally-compact-hausdorff-basics]]. A reader arriving from the other
    51	convention should read every unqualified "compact" here as "quasicompact".
    52	
    53	**2. Compactness of a subset is intrinsic.** [[def-compact-space]] calls
    54	$A \subseteq X$ compact when the subspace $A$ carries is a compact space, not when
    55	every family of open subsets of $X$ covering $A$ has finitely many members
    56	covering it. The two conditions agree, and that is a theorem,
    57	[[lem-compactness-of-a-subspace-is-ambient]]; no proof here uses the ambient
    58	reading without citing it. The point of the choice is that compactness then
    59	belongs to the space $A$ alone, so a set compact in one ambient space is compact
    60	in every other one in which it sits as a subspace. The metric development fixed
    61	the same reading, and that the two developments describe one notion is
    62	[[thm-compactness-agrees-with-metric-compactness]].
    63	
    64	**Relative compactness is the exception**, and deliberately so:
    65	[[def-compactness-variants]] defines $A$ to be relatively compact in $X$ when
    66	$\overline{A}$ is compact, and the closure is taken in $X$. That condition really
    67	is about $A$ inside $X$ and changes when the ambient space changes.
    68	
    69	**3. A separation axiom is written out rather than named where it is not
    70	available.** Claim 4 of [[thm-compactness-variants-hierarchy]] needs every
    71	singleton to be closed. That condition is a named separation axiom, and the page
    72	naming it is not among this page's declared prerequisites, so the hypothesis is
    73	stated in the vocabulary of open and closed sets and nothing is asserted about the
    74	axiom it belongs to. On this page a neighbourhood need not be open, and the
    75	intersection of no sets is the whole space; these two general conventions are in
    76	force without further comment.
    77	
    78	## The choice ledger
    79	
    80	Every entry below is a statement about **the proof given in this library**, and

