    26	  scraped: []
    27	  references:
    28	    - title: "Baire space (Wikipedia)"
    29	      url: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baire_space"
    30	    - title: "Baire category theorem (Wikipedia)"
    31	      url: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baire_category_theorem"
    32	pipeline_run: null
    33	---
    34	
    35	## Definition
    36	
    37	A topological space $(X, \mathcal{T})$ ([[def-topological-space]]) is a **Baire
    38	space** when
    39	
    40	> for every sequence $(U_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ of subsets of $X$ that are open
    41	> and dense in $X$ ([[def-dense-top]], [[def-sequence]],
    42	> [[def-natural-numbers]]), the intersection $\bigcap_{n \in \mathbb{N}} U_n$ is
    43	> dense in $X$.
    44	
    45	As everywhere in this library $\mathbb{N}$ contains $0$, so the sequence starts
    46	at $U_0$ and an index range copied from a text that starts at $1$ must be shifted
    47	before it is used here.
    48	
    49	**The condition unwound.** Denseness of $A \subseteq X$ says $\overline{A} = X$
    50	([[def-interior-closure-boundary-top]]), equivalently that $A$ meets every
    51	nonempty open subset of $X$ ([[def-dense-top]]). So the Baire condition reads:
    52	
    53	> for every sequence $(U_n)$ of dense open sets and every nonempty open
    54	> $W \subseteq X$ there is a point of $W$ lying in every $U_n$.
    55	
    56	That is the form every proof below uses, and it is the form in which the
    57	condition is checked: one produces a single point, in a given nonempty open set,
    58	belonging to all of the $U_n$ at once.
    59	
    60	**The intersection itself need not be open.** Nothing in the definition asserts
    61	that $\bigcap_n U_n$ is open, and in general it is not; what is asserted is only
    62	that it is dense. A finite intersection of dense open sets is dense and open, and
    63	that much holds in every space; the content of the definition is entirely in the
    64	passage to a countable family.
    65	
    66	**Why the meager formulation is not given here.** The usual equivalent phrasing
    67	says that a countable union of nowhere dense sets has empty interior, or that a
    68	nonempty open set is not meager. This page does not state it, because the
    69	notions of nowhere dense and meager available to it are
    70	[[def-nowhere-dense-meager]], which is stated for subsets of $\mathbb{R}$ and not
    71	for subsets of an arbitrary topological space; restating them here in general
    72	would create a second notion under the same name. The definition above is stated
    73	in terms of denseness alone, which [[def-dense-top]] does define for an arbitrary
    74	space, and it is equivalent to the meager formulation wherever both are
    75	available.

