    35	---
    36	
    37	## Statement
    38	
    39	**Assume the Axiom of Choice** ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]), in the form of Zorn's
    40	lemma ([[thm-zorn]]), the two being equivalent over ZF ([[cor-ac-iff-zorn]]).
    41	
    42	Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]) and let
    43	$\mathcal{S}$ be a subbasis for $\mathcal{T}$
    44	([[def-topology-basis-subbasis]]). Suppose that
    45	
    46	> every family $\mathcal{S}_0 \subseteq \mathcal{S}$ with
    47	> $X = \bigcup \mathcal{S}_0$ has a finite subfamily whose union is $X$.
    48	
    49	Then $(X, \mathcal{T})$ is compact ([[def-compact-space]]).
    50	
    51	The converse is immediate and is not the content: a compact space has a finite
    52	subcover for **every** open cover, subbasic or not. What the lemma says is that
    53	the subbasic covers alone already decide compactness, and that is what makes it
    54	usable — a product topology is presented by a subbasis, and the subbasic covers
    55	of a product are far easier to handle than its arbitrary open covers.
    56	
    57	## Facts & Assumptions
    58	
    59	**Given:** A topological space $(X, \mathcal{T})$, a subbasis $\mathcal{S}$ for $\mathcal{T}$, and the Axiom of Choice.
    60	
    61	[A1] Every family $\mathcal{S}_0 \subseteq \mathcal{S}$ with $X = \bigcup \mathcal{S}_0$ has a finite subfamily whose union is $X$.
    62	
    63	[L1] A space is compact exactly when every family of open sets with union the space has a finite subfamily with union the space, a family being finite when it is empty or listable as $\{V_0, \dots, V_n\}$ for some $n \in \mathbb{N}$; the empty space is compact ([[def-compact-space]]).
    64	
    65	[L2] Inclusion is a partial order on any family of sets, and a chain in it is a subfamily any two of whose members are comparable under inclusion ([[def-partial-order]], [[def-chain]]).
    66	
    67	[L3] Of finitely many pairwise comparable sets one contains all the others: for $\mathcal{D}_0, \dots, \mathcal{D}_n$ pairwise comparable, induction on $n$ gives such a member, the successor step comparing the member found for $\mathcal{D}_0, \dots, \mathcal{D}_n$ with $\mathcal{D}_{n+1}$ ([[def-chain]], [[thm-induction-principle]]).
    68	
    69	[L4] A function with domain a natural number all of whose values are nonempty sets has a choice function, and this is a theorem of ZF ([[lem-finite-choice]]).
    70	
    71	[L5] An upper bound of a subset of a poset is an element above all of its members ([[def-upper-bound]]); a maximal element is one with nothing strictly above it ([[def-maximal-element]]).
    72	
    73	[L6] Zorn's lemma: a nonempty poset in which every chain has an upper bound has a maximal element ([[thm-zorn]]).
    74	
    75	[L7] The intersections of finitely many members of $\mathcal{S}$ form a basis for $\mathcal{T}$, the intersection of none being $X$; and for a basis $\mathcal{B}$, every open $O$ and every $x \in O$ admit $B \in \mathcal{B}$ with $x \in B \subseteq O$ ([[thm-basis-criterion]], claim 2; [[def-topology-basis-subbasis]]).
    76	
    77	[L8] An arbitrary union of open sets is open, and so is an intersection of finitely many open sets when at least one is taken ([[def-topological-space]]).
    78	
    79	## Proof
    80	
    81	**Proof technique:** contradiction.
    82	
    83	1.1 Suppose $(X, \mathcal{T})$ is not compact. Then $X \ne \varnothing$, the empty space being compact by [L1], and the family $\mathcal{P}$ of those open covers of $X$ that have no finite subcover is a nonempty subfamily of the power set of $\mathcal{T}$, partially ordered by inclusion. [L1, L2, assume-contra]
    84	
    85	2.1 Every chain $\mathcal{C} \subseteq \mathcal{P}$ has an upper bound in $\mathcal{P}$. For $\mathcal{C} = \varnothing$ any member of $\mathcal{P}$ is an upper bound, and $\mathcal{P}$ is nonempty by step 1.1. For $\mathcal{C} \ne \varnothing$ take $\mathcal{B} := \bigcup \mathcal{C}$, a family of open sets whose union is $X$ because the union of any one member of $\mathcal{C}$ already is; were $\mathcal{B}$ to have a finite subcover $U_0, \dots, U_n$, then for each $j \le n$ the set of members of $\mathcal{C}$ containing $U_j$ is nonempty, [L4] would supply $\mathcal{D}_0, \dots, \mathcal{D}_n \in \mathcal{C}$ with $U_j \in \mathcal{D}_j$, and [L3] would put all of them inside one $\mathcal{D} \in \mathcal{C}$, which would then have the finite subcover $U_0, \dots, U_n$ and could not lie in $\mathcal{P}$. So $\mathcal{B} \in \mathcal{P}$, and it contains every member of $\mathcal{C}$. [L2, L3, L4, L5, step 1.1]

